<title>USA: Johnson &amp; Higgins declines comment on report.</title>
<headline>Johnson &amp; Higgins declines comment on report.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-03-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Johnson &amp; Higgins declined comment on a published report that it is considering going public or selling itself, as the insurance brokerage industry struggles with falling revenues and demand.</p>
<p>The Wall Street Journal, quoting people familiar with the matter, said Johnson &amp; Higgins has hired Morgan Stanley to explore its strategic options.</p>
<p>Morgan Stanley was not available for comment.</p>
<p>The report said J&amp;H has been talking to several companies about combinations, including Marsh &amp; McLennan Cos Inc.</p>
<p>Marsh &amp; McLennan also declined comment.</p>
<p>The newspaper said J&amp;H also was believed to have talked recently to at least one insurance carrier, American International Group Inc, about a combination.</p>
<p>American International Group declined comment.</p>
<p>Industry analysts said other possible acquirers included Sedgwick Group Plc, the newspaper said.</p>
<p>Sedgwick declined comment.</p>
</text>
